WEBVTT

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:12.175
Bee Tracker follows every bee in an arena, live from a camera or from a saved video, and turns the tracks into distances, speeds, zone times and social contact.

00:00:12.176 --> 00:00:18.308
This demo uses a simulated camera with three bees, so you can follow along without any hardware.

00:00:18.308 --> 00:00:22.717
Start with the session details: who you are, and what the experiment is.

00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:27.561
Then press Start camera. If you have more than one camera, choose it first.

00:00:27.561 --> 00:00:31.630
Press Draw arena, and drag a box around the arena floor.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:38.189
Enter the arena's real width in millimetres. That sets the scale, so distances come out in millimetres.

00:00:38.189 --> 00:00:41.019
Then tell it how many bees are in the arena.

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:46.645
Zones mark places you care about, like a feeder. Press Add zone, and drag a box.

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:53.508
Click a zone's outline to select it. You can rename it, drag it into place, or resize it with the handles.

00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:57.876
An edge band measures time near the wall. Here, ten millimetres.

00:00:57.876 --> 00:01:02.455
Now the Detect tab. With the bees in the arena, press Set up detection.

00:01:02.455 --> 00:01:08.529
It learns the background while the bees move, then works out the threshold, and the size of one bee.

00:01:08.529 --> 00:01:16.402
It found all three bees. The magenta shows what it detects. If it ever gets something wrong, adjust it under Advanced.

00:01:16.403 --> 00:01:24.096
The LEDs tab plays a light protocol on the DAQ with each trial. The desktop app connects to it for you.

00:01:24.097 --> 00:01:31.428
Each segment sets what both LEDs do. Here: dark, then blue on, dark again, then a green flicker.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:38.193
Test LEDs turns each one on at five volts for a second, so you can check the wiring.

00:01:38.193 --> 00:01:49.095
Everything for a trial sits under the video. Set the length, here thirty seconds, and press Start trial.

00:01:49.095 --> 00:01:55.759
Each bee gets a number and a coloured track. The red timer shows how far the trial has got.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.532
The LED protocol plays on the DAQ's own clock, lined up with the camera frames.

00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:08.431
Keep this tab in front while it tracks: browsers slow down tabs that are hidden.

00:02:08.431 --> 00:02:12.209
When the trial ends, the results fill in straight away.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:19.123
Each bee gets a row: distance walked, speed, time moving, time in each zone, and time near the wall.

00:02:19.124 --> 00:02:28.657
With more than one bee, Near bee is the share of time within ten millimetres of another bee, with the number of encounters. The pair table breaks it down.

00:02:28.658 --> 00:02:40.066
Beside the map of every track, two charts follow the trial over time: distance from the food, and distance between the bees. The shaded bands are the LED conditions.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:45.252
Point at a chart, and the map shows where each bee was at that moment.

00:02:45.252 --> 00:02:56.236
The Data tab saves the tracks and summaries as CSV files, or exports to PER Scorer. Save the project file at the end of each session.

00:02:56.237 --> 00:03:00.705
Each new trial becomes its own recording, ready to compare between groups.

00:03:00.705 --> 00:03:08.296
That's it: draw the arena, set up detection, start the trial, and read the results.
